<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yennenga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yennenga is a legendary princess of Mamprugu, honoured as the mother of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>. Tradition tells that she rode away from her father's court, met a hunter named Riale and bore a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouedraogo">Ouedraogo</a>, the founder of the Mossi kingdoms. Her image on horseback is a national symbol of Burkina Faso, and the top prize of the FESPACO film festival bears her name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yennenga.jpg" alt="Yennenga" width="265" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Women in African History: an E-Learning Tool; CC-BY-SA UNESCO/Brenda Gael McSweeney · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yennega.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
